## How Trump Accounts Became Part of the SpaceX Pledge

No official source states that you must claim a [Trump Account](https://trumpaccounts.gov) to receive the [SpaceX](https://www.spacex.com/) stock donation, nor has any agency confirmed whether claiming an account makes it active. When [Elon Musk](https://x.com/elonmusk) first posted about the gift on October 19, 2026, he pledged one share to every child in America without specifying a distribution vehicle. Some market coverage assumed Trump Accounts would be used because of precedent set earlier in the year.

In July 2026, SpaceX President Gwynne Shotwell pledged one share each to the Trump Accounts of roughly two million children, according to [The Next Web](https://thenextweb.com/news/shotwell-spacex-stock-trump-accounts). The federal system also offered an established roster of American minors. The [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0642) completed automatic enrollment for eligible children without existing accounts on October 1, 2026, under press release SB-0642.

On November 25, 2026, an official [SpaceX release](https://ir.spacex.com/updates/releases-details/2026/SpaceX-to-Invest-in-the-Next-Generation-of-Americans-2026-r_LYQ5cH94/default.aspx) confirmed that the company will give one newly issued Class A share to every currently active Trump Account, with Musk personally matching each grant. That announcement did not define active status, set a distribution timetable, or outline a claim mandate. Reporting from [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/25/spacex-musk-trump-account-stock.html) noted that while the White House cited nearly 70 million accounts created by October, it remains unconfirmed how many qualify as active.

Claiming an auto-enrolled account remains an administrative process handled directly through the official app or at trumpaccounts.gov. Parents and guardians must claim an account to unlock the one-time $1,000 federal seed deposit for eligible birth years and allow private family or employer contributions. CNBC reported that auto-enrolled accounts can accept general philanthropic contributions without a parental claim, but neither SpaceX nor Treasury has stated whether the SpaceX donation follows that path.

Recipients face statutory transfer limits on any shares they receive. CNBC, citing Treasury rules, reported that families cannot refuse stock gifts, and the shares generally cannot be sold until the earlier of five years or the end of the calendar year in which the beneficiary turns 17. If you want to manage family deposits or check an existing profile, review our walkthrough on [how to claim a Trump Account](/guides/how-to-claim-a-trump-account/).